\description{
Takes the sheet \code{\link[=coding_sample]{coding_sample()}} wrote, with \code{human_label} filled in, and
reports how well the model agreed with the coder.

The sheet carries no probabilities, which is what makes it blind. This joins
back to the results on \code{id} and \code{item} to recover them, so pass the same
results object the sample came from.

\code{vignette("complete_run")} works a run end to end.
}
\section{Several coders}{

\code{sheet} takes a named list as well as one sheet, so
\code{validate_sample(list(ana = ana, luis = luis), res)} scores the model against
each coder in one call and adds \verb{$ceiling} and \verb{$gap}. Each coder is scored
through the one-sheet path, so no figure is computed twice or computed
differently, and the model tables gain a \code{coder} column after \code{item}.

Coders need not have worked every row. \verb{$ceiling$alpha} uses every row two
or more coders reached, and each pair in \verb{$ceiling$pairs} falls back to the
rows both of them saw, so the two tables can rest on different counts.

\verb{$gap} resamples whatever \code{ci} is set to, since the gap is read through its
interval. Every replicate redraws the documents once and recomputes both
sides on that draw, because the two share the documents they rest on.

A list of one is refused. A sheet \code{\link[=coder_reference]{coder_reference()}} consolidated warns when
it is placed in a list, since agreeing with a consensus is easier than
agreeing with one person.
}

\section{Ordered labels}{

Ordered labels need \code{level = "ordinal"}. The default charges every
disagreement the same, which is right for unordered labels and wrong for a
scale, where two coders one step apart have agreed more than two coders at
opposite ends. Pass \code{labels} in scale order, least to greatest, or an
\code{instrument} carrying \code{scores}. Nothing is inferred, because no order can be
recovered from the sheets.
}

\section{A soft reference}{

A sheet carrying \code{human_prop} instead of \code{human_label} is scored as a
measurement and not as a classification. There is no second categorical
variable, so \verb{$discrimination} and \verb{$categories} are not returned. What comes
back is \verb{$calibration}, with \code{brier} against the proportion, whatever
\code{calibration} is set to.
}

\examples{
# Results from a real run ship with the package, so this needs no server.
p <- system.file("extdata", "vignette_results.rds", package = "fathom")
baked <- readRDS(p)
res <- baked$res_six
sheet <- coding_sample(res, parlasent, text = "sentence", id = "id", n = 20,
  seed = 1234)

# The parlasent coders, standing in for someone filling the sheet in.
six <- c("Negative", "M_Negative", "N_Neutral", "P_Neutral", "M_Positive",
  "Positive")
coded <- function(who) {
  out <- sheet
  out$human_label <- as.character(
    match(parlasent[[who]][match(sheet$id, parlasent$id)], six)
  )
  out
}
a1 <- coded("annotator1")

v <- validate_sample(a1, res, level = "ordinal", labels = as.character(1:6))
v$discrimination
v$categories

# A second coder adds the ceiling the two set between themselves, and the
# model against it.
a2 <- coded("annotator2")
v2 <- validate_sample(list(annotator1 = a1, annotator2 = a2), res,
  level = "ordinal", labels = as.character(1:6), bootstrap_n = 200)
v2$ceiling$pairs
v2$gap
}
